/P 485 0 R
/E (Process shows work of the system. Each process has one or more data input sand produces one or more data outputs. Processes are represented by circles in DFDs. )
/Pg 51 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
1410 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 485 0 R
/E (Data stores are the repositories of data. Processes can enter data into the store or retrieve the data from the data store. Data stores are represented by parallel lines which may be depicted horizontally or vertically. )
/Pg 51 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
1411 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 485 0 R
/E (The top-level diagram is often called a "context diagram". It contains a single process, but it plays a very important role in studying the current system. The context diagram defines the system that will be studied in the sense that it determines the boundaries. Dataflow Diagrams are made up of a number of symbols which represent system components. Dataflow modelling uses four kinds of symbols. )
/Pg 51 0 R
/K [11 12]
>>
endobj
1412 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 485 0 R
/E ( Representing a system at any level of detail with a graphic network of symbols showing data flows, data stores, data process, sources or destination. The DFD is designed to aid communication. DFD shows the minimum contents of data stores. In order to show what happens with in a given process, then the detailed explosion of that process is shown. The DFD methodology is quite effective, especially when the required design is unclear and the user and the analyst need a notational language for communication. )
/Pg 51 0 R
/K [13 14]
>>
endobj
